Dynamic bottleneck prediction can warn manufacturers of emerging throughput constraints and guide resource adjustment before production is disrupted; timely prediction is therefore urgently needed to secure sufficient lead time for effective intervention.
Existing studies mainly rely on single-entity data returned by machine or workstation sensors, with limited representation of workers, materials, buffers, and logistics and of their relations.
This narrow observation scope leads to incomplete factory-graph modelling, reduces prediction accuracy, and makes it difficult to capture disturbances that propagate across resources after a delay.
A second limitation is the difficulty of constructing reliable bottleneck supervision: positive events are scarce, whereas isolated threshold crossings may conflate transient fluctuations with sustained constraints and obscure their process causes.
To address these limitations, we propose the \textbf{Process-Informed Heterogeneous-Graph Spatiotemporal Transformer with Delayed Propagation (PHGST-DP)}.
To address the first limitation, PHGST-DP combines a heterogeneous process--logistics graph with prototype-conditioned delay-aware propagation, linking earlier resource states to constraints that emerge elsewhere after transport, buffering, or service delays.
To address the second, a disturbance-controlled digital production line provides traceable trajectories; process-informed rules, persistence filtering, and training-only clustering construct sustained event and cause targets and recurrent operating-pattern prototypes.
In addition, the model jointly outputs bottleneck station, onset, duration, process cause, and remaining order time as a decision-oriented event report.
Held-out digital-twin experiments demonstrate high bottleneck-prediction accuracy and timely report generation, while ablations support the effectiveness of the proposed components.
These results support PHGST-DP under the studied digital-twin conditions and indicate its potential for early warning and resource coordination.

